# 13. Working with `hql`

The language is half of what you use; the other half is the command and what
it says back. This chapter is about the tools: the commands, the diagnostics,
and the messages `hql` writes when a program is wrong.

## The commands

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `hql eval <program>` | check and evaluate a program given as an argument |
| `hql run <file>` | check and evaluate a program in a file; `-` reads standard input |
| `hql check <file>` | write the program's type, and the signature of every alias, without evaluating |
| `hql repl` | read, evaluate and print, keeping bindings between lines |
| `hql render <file>` | build a document from its cells ([chapter 24](24-documents.hmd)) |
| `hql builtins` | list every step, by the provider that implements it, with its signature |
| `hql config` | the settings in force, and where each came from |
| `hql context [file]` | the execution context: the mode, the workspace, the configuration files and module roots, and where each came from |
| `hql doctor` | check the semantic-search chain and name the first missing part |

`--vault <dir>` names the vault, `--to <format>` the output format, and
`--envelope json` wraps the answer for another program. `--workspace <dir>`,
`--bare` and `--context <name>` set the execution context, below.

## The REPL

`hql repl` keeps its bindings from one input to the next. The arrow keys edit a
line and recall earlier inputs, from a history kept between sessions. An input
goes on over several lines while a bracket is open, or while the next line
opens with `|`, so every program in this book can be pasted in as it is
written. `:type` asks for the type of an expression, or, given a function's
name, prints the standard library's declarations of it, one per overload.
`:quit` leaves, as do `quit`, `exit` and Ctrl-D:

```text
$ hql repl
hql 0.5.1. `:help` for commands, `:quit` to leave.
hql> shelf = [412, 280]
()
hql> shelf | sum
692
hql> :type shelf
List<Int>
hql> :type range
fn range(start: Int, end: Int, step: Int = 1) : List<Int>    // std/seq
hql> :quit
```

## What a step takes

`hql builtins` lists every step with its signature, one line per form,
grouped by the module declaring it. Each heading names the module, the
package providing it and whether a program has it without an import:

```text
$ hql builtins
collections (std 0.6.0, always available)
  size
    Collection<T> | size<T>() -> Int
    Window<T> | size<T>() -> Int
    pure, reduction
    The number of element occurrences in `self`, an `Int`: `[1, 2, 2, 3] | size` is `4`, and `Set([1, 2, 2]) | size` is `2`.
  contains
    Collection<T> | contains<T>(value: T (positional)) -> Bool
    pure, reduction
    `true` when `value` occurs in `self`, and `false` otherwise: `[1, 2, 3] | contains(2)` is `true`.
  …
```

A function of a module that the program has not imported is listed too, so
the import it needs can be looked up. `import lexical` makes a module visible;
`lexical.lexical(…)` names a function with its module, when two visible modules
declare one name and the input does not tell them apart. The modules of
`lib/std/prelude.hql`, `string`, `seq`, `math`, `option`, `result`, `graph`,
`format` and `io` among them, are visible in every program unless the vault's
`hql.toml` says `prelude = false`.

## Diagnostics

A run produces a value **and** the diagnostics its phases wrote: warnings that
change nothing, errors that make a result wrong, failures that make one
impossible. Each is headed by the phase that wrote it, its severity and its
kind, and names where it points, by line and column.
[[12-errors|Chapter 12]] is the whole error model.

By default `hql` stops at the first error. `--report collect` reports every
error it can find:

```text
$ hql --report collect eval 'a = nosuchthing
b = alsomissing'
check error[unknown-name]: `nosuchthing` is not bound
 --> <expression>:1:5
  |
1 | a = nosuchthing
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^

check error[unknown-name]: `alsomissing` is not bound
 --> <expression>:2:5
  |
2 | b = alsomissing
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^

2 diagnostics: 0 failure, 2 error, 0 warning
```

A misspelled step suggests the nearest one, and a retired name says what
replaced it, on a `help` line under the caret:

```text
$ hql eval '[1, 2] | mapp(n => n)'
check error[unknown-step]: `mapp` is not a pipeline step
 --> <expression>:1:10
  |
1 | [1, 2] | mapp(n => n)
  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = help: did you mean `map`?
```

A warning never changes the exit status. Names have one casing per kind —
`snake_case` for bindings, functions and fields, `PascalCase` for types — and
a name off its kind's convention is a warning where it is written:

```text
$ hql eval 'bookCount = 7
bookCount'
7
check warning[naming-convention]: `bookCount` is a binding, written in snake_case
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | bookCount = 7
  | ^^^^^^^^^
  = help: write it `book_count`
```

`--envelope json` carries every diagnostic, whatever the level, as data, and
`--diagnostics-format json` writes each one to standard error as one JSON
document per line. A program that wants to report on itself, apart from its
result, writes to a logger: the [[09-io/logging|logging page]] of chapter 9.

## The execution context

Every invocation runs in an **execution context**: a mode, a workspace or
none, and the configuration gathered for it. The same program text checks
and evaluates the same way in every mode; the context determines the
documents, module roots and settings it is given.

### Modes and workspace discovery

The **anchor** is the directory discovery starts from: the directory of the
program's file for `hql run`, `check`, `plan`, `boundary` and `context <file>`, and the
working directory for an expression or standard input. `hql` walks from the
anchor upward and stops at the first directory holding `.hmd/` or
`hql.toml`; that directory is the **workspace**. The mode follows from what
it holds:

| Mode | Workspace | Vault |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `bare` | none | none |
| `workspace` | the directory holding `hql.toml` | none |
| `vault` | the directory holding `.hmd/`, with or without `hql.toml` | the workspace |

The walk stops at the nearest boundary: a `hql.toml` inside a vault is a
workspace of its own, and the outer vault's configuration does not reach it.
The working directory is not consulted when a file is the anchor, so a
program finds its workspace wherever `hql` is run from. A workspace marked by
`hql.toml` alone still loads its documents as cards.

`--workspace <dir>` (or `--vault <dir>`) names the workspace and replaces the
walk; the directory must hold `.hmd/` or `hql.toml`. `--bare` runs with no
workspace whatever the filesystem holds, and an operation that needs a vault
fails with `needs a vault` rather than reading the working directory as one.
`--bare` with `--workspace` is a usage error. `hql render` discovers a
workspace for each document from that document's directory, and under
`--bare` renders none.

`hql context` writes the context, each part with the layer it came from:

```text
$ cd ~/tmp
$ hql context ../notes/scripts/report.hql
mode:      vault
context:   work (user configuration)
workspace: /home/ada/notes (discovered from the anchor)
vault:     /home/ada/notes
anchor:    /home/ada/notes/scripts
cwd:       /home/ada/tmp
hql-home:  /home/ada/.hql (the default)
prelude:   on (the default)
config:
  /etc/hql/config.toml (system configuration, absent)
  /home/ada/.hql/config.toml (user configuration)
  /home/ada/notes/hql.toml (the workspace's hql.toml, absent)
module roots:
  /home/ada/.hql/lib (user configuration)
imports:
  none
```

`--envelope json` writes the same as one JSON document.

### Configuration layers

Bare means no workspace, not no configuration. These layers contribute in
every mode, each read in turn:

| Layer | Where |
| --- | --- |
| system | `/etc/hql/config.toml` on Unix, `%ProgramData%\hql\config.toml` on Windows; `$HQL_SYSTEM_CONFIG` names another file |
| user | `config.toml` in the **HQL home**, `~/.hql/`; `$HQL_HOME` names another directory |
| named context | the selected `[context.<name>]` table of the system or user file |
| HQL home modules | `~/.hql/modules/`, a module root when it exists |
| workspace | the workspace's `hql.toml` |
| `$HQL_PATH` | further module roots, separated as `$PATH` is |
| command line | `--modules <dir>` |

The system and user files take the keys a workspace's `hql.toml` takes:
`[modules] roots`, `[extensions] import` and `prelude`, `[report] mode` and
`level`, and `[execution] backend`. A relative path is read against the
directory of the file that declares it, never the working directory. A
system or user file that does not parse fails every run, naming the file.

Module roots and extension imports gather across the layers, each once. A
gathered root is a place to look, not a priority: a module that two roots
provide is still an error. `prelude` is the last layer's that states it. The
HQL home is the user's environment, not a workspace, and never a vault.

### Named contexts

A **named context** is a `[context.<name>]` table of the system or user file:
settings grouped under a name and reused across workspaces. It takes the
keys above, and `workspace`, which binds a workspace, read against the
directory of the declaring file. A user file's definition of a name replaces
the system file's; a workspace's `hql.toml` defines no context.

```toml
current-context = "work"

[modules]
roots = ["lib"]

[context.work.report]
level = "info"

[context.archive]
workspace = "../archive"
```

One context is selected: `--context <name>`, else `$HQL_CONTEXT`, else
`current-context`, else a context named `default` when one is defined. A
selected name that no file defines fails every run, naming the layer that
selected it. A context that binds a workspace replaces the walk; a context
without one leaves discovery as it is. A context selected by `--context` or
`$HQL_CONTEXT` that binds a workspace, together with `--bare` or a
`--workspace` naming another directory, is an error; under a context selected
by `current-context`, the command line wins.

`hql config` lists and changes the selection; `use-context` writes
`current-context` into the user file and keeps every other line of it:

```text
$ hql config get-contexts
CURRENT  NAME     WORKSPACE          DEFINED IN
         archive  /home/ada/archive  /home/ada/.hql/config.toml
*        work     -                  /home/ada/.hql/config.toml
$ hql --context archive context
mode:      vault
context:   archive (--context)
workspace: /home/ada/archive (context `archive`)
…
$ hql --context nope eval 1
hql: no context is named `nope`, which --context selects; the defined contexts are `archive`, `work`
```

`hql config current-context` writes the persistent `current-context`,
which `--context` and `$HQL_CONTEXT` leave unchanged, `hql config
use-context <name>` sets it, and `hql config view` writes the system and
user files merged, a table key by key and any other value from the user
file.

## Settings

The report mode and level come from the command line, then the vault's
`hql.toml`, then the environment (`$HQL_REPORT`, `$HQL_REPORT_LEVEL`), then the
selected context, the user file and the system file, then the defaults. The
backend follows the same order with `--backend`, `[execution] backend` and
`$HQL_BACKEND`. `hql config` says which applies, and why:

```text
$ hql config
report mode: strict (from the default)
report level: warning (from the default)
backend: localhost (from the default)
vault: none
```

`backend` selects the execution strategy described in
[[14-planning-and-backends|Logical steps and physical plans]], and
`localhost` is the only one. Run inside a vault, or with `--vault`, the last
line names it and counts its cards.

## What is next

Every chapter so far was about a step. [[14-etl-pipelines|Chapter 14]] is about
the pipeline the steps form: what `|` does, when a program is checked, where
it runs, and the systems a pipeline will connect.
